WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.916
Ansible runs on one machine, called the control node.

2
00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:07.919
In this course, that is your Mac.

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.973
First, it reads the inventory.

4
00:00:09.973 --> 00:00:15.187
This file lists your servers, sorts them into groups, and says how to reach each one.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:22.305
The lab has three practice servers: web1 and web2 in the web group, and lb1 in the lb group.

6
00:00:22.305 --> 00:00:26.208
They share one address, and differ only by SSH port.

7
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:31.112
Each host becomes a managed node: a server that Ansible configures.

8
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:35.351
To work on them, Ansible logs in to each one over SSH.

9
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:41.603
Nothing of Ansible is installed on these servers, and no Ansible process waits there.

10
00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:43.663
That is what agentless means.

11
00:00:43.663 --> 00:00:47.853
A server needs only two things: an SSH login, and Python 3.

12
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.069
The ping module checks both.

13
00:00:50.069 --> 00:00:55.717
It logs in to every server, and each one answers pong when SSH and Python work.

14
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.252
Your playbook lists tasks.

15
00:00:58.252 --> 00:01:02.349
Each task calls one module, and describes the result you want.

16
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:07.096
Here, the apt module, and the result: tree and htop must be present.

17
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.839
These are fresh servers.

18
00:01:08.839 --> 00:01:13.201
tree and htop are missing, and the login message is not written yet.

19
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:14.574
Run the playbook.

20
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:19.271
Ansible takes the first task, and does the same steps on every server.

21
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.509
It copies a small Python program, the module, to each server.

22
00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:28.175
By default it works on five servers at a time, so all three go at once.

23
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.018
The server's python3 runs the module.

24
00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:33.777
And the module checks the server first.

25
00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:35.759
Are tree and htop installed?

26
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:37.022
They are not.

27
00:01:37.022 --> 00:01:38.647
So it installs them.

28
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:44.868
Then the module prints a small JSON result with a changed field, and Ansible reads it back.

29
00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:46.517
Here, changed, in yellow.

30
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:49.273
The next task writes the login message.

31
00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:54.939
The copy module checks the file, finds it is not right, writes it, and reports changed.

32
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.544
At the end comes the PLAY RECAP, one line per server.

33
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:02.146
ok counts every task that succeeded, changed or not.

34
00:02:02.146 --> 00:02:05.265
changed counts the tasks that changed something.

35
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:11.352
web1 has three ok: our two tasks, plus gathering facts, which starts every play.

36
00:02:11.352 --> 00:02:16.723
lb1 also ran a second play that sets up nginx, so its numbers are bigger.

37
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:20.503
Now run the same playbook again, without changing anything.

38
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:22.415
The module checks first.

39
00:02:22.415 --> 00:02:24.936
tree and htop are already installed.

40
00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:27.388
So it does nothing, and reports ok.

41
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.684
The same for the login message.

42
00:02:29.684 --> 00:02:32.776
The file already says the right thing, so: ok.

43
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.527
The recap says changed equals zero, on every server.

44
00:02:36.733 --> 00:02:43.571
lb1 shows eight ok instead of nine, because its nginx reload runs only after a change.

45
00:02:43.571 --> 00:02:45.877
This time, nothing called it.

46
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.362
This run took about ten seconds, and changed nothing.

47
00:02:49.362 --> 00:02:51.577
That property is called idempotence.

48
00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:55.208
It is the main thing Ansible gives you over a shell script.

49
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:59.390
The shell and command modules cannot know what a command did.

50
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.595
So they report changed every time.

51
00:03:01.595 --> 00:03:03.411
Treat them as a last resort.

52
00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:08.717
Now someone fixes web2 by hand, and adds a line to its login message.

53
00:03:08.933 --> 00:03:10.741
Ansible keeps no state file.

54
00:03:10.741 --> 00:03:15.325
Every run checks the real server, so the next run finds the difference.

55
00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:19.465
It puts the file back, and shows exactly the line it removed.

56
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:23.554
A difference between the code and the server is called drift.

57
00:03:23.554 --> 00:03:26.485
A regular Ansible run finds it, and undoes it.

58
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.294
So each module checks first, and changes only what differs.

59
00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:34.767
That is why a second run is safe, and changes nothing.
